WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.829 --> 00:00:04.930
RUBIN: Hi.

00:00:04.930 --> 00:00:09.730
Welcome to this third session of today's Council
on Foreign Relations symposium on "The New

00:00:09.730 --> 00:00:13.530
Geopolitics of China, India, and Pakistan."

00:00:13.530 --> 00:00:18.289
It's entitled-this session is entitled "U.S.
Policy Options to Stabilize the Region."

00:00:18.289 --> 00:00:22.430
I'll remind you all that this session is on
the record.

00:00:22.430 --> 00:00:30.000
It is being broadcast live on the-it's being
webcast live through the CFR website, cfr.org,

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:35.020
and there are two hashtags-and you're actually
encouraged to tweet about it in the course

00:00:35.020 --> 00:00:41.210
of the meeting-#NewGeopolitics and #CFRLive.

00:00:41.210 --> 00:00:47.580
And I just wanted to, before I introduce the
panelists, mention that, where I work, the

00:00:47.580 --> 00:00:52.059
Center on International Cooperation at New
York University, has published a report which

00:00:52.059 --> 00:00:58.920
might be of interest to those who are here
entitled "The New Silk Roads: China, the U.S.,

00:00:58.920 --> 00:01:00.680
and the Future of Central Asia."

00:01:00.680 --> 00:01:03.159
I was very negligent in not bringing any copies
of it.

00:01:03.159 --> 00:01:04.320
I forgot about it.

00:01:04.320 --> 00:01:07.350
Anyway, but you can-if you are interested
in it, you can find it.

00:01:07.350 --> 00:01:10.230
Well, let me briefly introduce the panelists.

00:01:10.230 --> 00:01:12.850
You have their biographies in front of you.

00:01:12.850 --> 00:01:18.280
Marc Grossman, first and foremost, my former
boss when he was the special representative

00:01:18.280 --> 00:01:20.270
for Afghanistan and Pakistan in the State
Department.

00:01:20.270 --> 00:01:21.830
He came back from retirement.

00:01:21.830 --> 00:01:25.900
Before that he had been undersecretary for
political affairs.

00:01:25.900 --> 00:01:26.900
Is that right, "P"?

00:01:26.900 --> 00:01:27.900
Right.

00:01:27.900 --> 00:01:28.900
GROSSMAN: Correct.

00:01:28.900 --> 00:01:32.310
RUBIN: Right, yes, and a variety of distinguished
posts including ambassador to Turkey.

00:01:32.310 --> 00:01:37.990
Alyssa Ayres, of course, is one of the authors
of this project, senior fellow at the Council

00:01:37.990 --> 00:01:43.120
on Foreign Relations, former deputy assistant
secretary of state for South Asia.

00:01:43.120 --> 00:01:49.920
And actually, in my time in State, I recall
both of us participating in the session on

00:01:49.920 --> 00:01:53.880
South Asia of the strategic dialogue with
China.

00:01:53.880 --> 00:01:57.190
So our work on these relationships goes back
a ways.

00:01:57.190 --> 00:02:05.490
And Evan Medeiros, who is the managing director
and practice head at the Eurasia Group, and

00:02:05.490 --> 00:02:10.940
formerly worked in the White House as special
assistant for the president on Asian affairs.

00:02:10.940 --> 00:02:11.940
Thank you.

00:02:11.940 --> 00:02:16.910
Now, I just wanted to make a couple opening
comments sort of to frame the discussion before

00:02:16.910 --> 00:02:21.200
turning to the panelists.

00:02:21.200 --> 00:02:25.980
One of the things that I worked on with Ambassador
Grossman in my time in the State Department

00:02:25.980 --> 00:02:32.930
was doing the diplomatic preparatory work
for the establishment of the Istanbul conference,

00:02:32.930 --> 00:02:35.010
now also known as the Heart of Asia process-Istanbul.

00:02:35.010 --> 00:02:44.750
But prior to that, to the formal conference,
there was a track 1.5 session, a track 1.5

00:02:44.750 --> 00:02:45.950
series of meetings.

00:02:45.950 --> 00:02:53.099
And at one of them, the Turkish representative
said that he would like to see the Istanbul

00:02:53.099 --> 00:03:00.160
process turn into something like-it's only
for Afghanistan-like the Balkan Stability

00:03:00.160 --> 00:03:04.760
Pact in Europe, which Turkey was involved
with and in which he had been involved with.

00:03:04.760 --> 00:03:12.230
He said: What's missing is the incentive,
the power of attraction of joining the EU

00:03:12.230 --> 00:03:18.819
or joining NATO, which gave economic incentives
for the countries in the Balkans to settle

00:03:18.819 --> 00:03:25.180
or put aside their various political and security
differences for economic benefit.

00:03:25.180 --> 00:03:31.129
And I think-the second observation is really
the same one-in the course of the discussion

00:03:31.129 --> 00:03:40.160
this morning, if I understand it correctly,
people from all these three countries-India,

00:03:40.160 --> 00:03:49.599
Pakistan, and China-have talked about the
rapidly growing potential economic benefits

00:03:49.599 --> 00:03:52.090
to cooperation.

00:03:52.090 --> 00:03:59.140
Nonetheless, their relationships in the past
and the way that the United States has related

00:03:59.140 --> 00:04:07.270
to them have primarily been driven by security
issues.

00:04:07.270 --> 00:04:14.520
And it's those security issues and political
issues that are the major factors in the conflict

00:04:14.520 --> 00:04:19.849
in Afghanistan, which is the hottest conflict
in many other disputes among these countries.

00:04:19.849 --> 00:04:28.780
So the big question to think about is, I think,
can we envisage how-not in the next year or

00:04:28.780 --> 00:04:36.110
even the next administration, but over time-the
relationships among these countries might

00:04:36.110 --> 00:04:42.490
be driven by those cooperative economic interests,
and, given that the focus on this panel is

00:04:42.490 --> 00:04:47.370
on U.S. policy, how the U.S. might facilitate
that?

00:04:47.370 --> 00:04:54.059
And just a final point-which Alyssa and I
have talked about this as recently as yesterday,

00:04:54.059 --> 00:04:58.819
actually-that of course the administration
has talked about a pivot to Asia, which China

00:04:58.819 --> 00:05:03.449
has seen as potentially threatening.

00:05:03.449 --> 00:05:08.219
But at the same time that the U.S. is pivoting
to Asia-which basically means the Asia-Pacific

00:05:08.219 --> 00:05:16.099
region-China is pivoting to Eurasia, to the
West, which to some extent the U.S. is disengaging

00:05:16.099 --> 00:05:18.460
from as it disengages from Afghanistan.

00:05:18.460 --> 00:05:25.289
So I think part of the agenda of the whole
project is to promote a broader notion of

00:05:25.289 --> 00:05:30.850
what it means to focus on Asia as the center
of economic dynamism in the world today.

00:05:30.850 --> 00:05:37.499
So let me first ask Marc-you, of course, can
and will talk about anything that you like,

00:05:37.499 --> 00:05:42.689
but-(laughter)-I thought one of the things
that we observed changing during the time

00:05:42.689 --> 00:05:53.499
that you were in office was, in particular,
the interest of China in Afghanistan particularly.

00:05:53.499 --> 00:05:59.189
You had to deal with that both bilaterally
and also in your dealings with India and Afghanistan

00:05:59.189 --> 00:06:00.619
as well.

00:06:00.619 --> 00:06:05.240
And also it became an issue in some of the
multilateral initiatives that you were involved

00:06:05.240 --> 00:06:06.240
in shepherding.

00:06:06.240 --> 00:06:09.009
So I wonder if you could say how you saw those
things developing.

00:06:09.009 --> 00:06:10.710
GROSSMAN: Well, first of all, thank you very
much.

00:06:10.710 --> 00:06:14.139
I appreciate the introduction that you gave
and I thank you very much for the time that

00:06:14.139 --> 00:06:16.309
we served together.

00:06:16.309 --> 00:06:22.990
I'm really lucky here in the sense that, out
of everything that I have to say, it was really

00:06:22.990 --> 00:06:26.939
talked about by the panel before, which is
to say that the short answer to this question,

00:06:26.939 --> 00:06:32.860
from my perspective, is that change in U.S.
policy-U.S. policy to do more-would be to

00:06:32.860 --> 00:06:37.150
focus in on all the kinds of things, Barney,
that you said in your introduction and that

00:06:37.150 --> 00:06:38.639
the last panel talked about.

00:06:38.639 --> 00:06:41.509
And of course there's a foundation for all
of these things.

00:06:41.509 --> 00:06:47.069
You were kind enough to mention the Istanbul
process, but this idea that there are questions

00:06:47.069 --> 00:06:51.560
of physical security, there are political
questions, all these things are tied up, it

00:06:51.560 --> 00:06:54.800
seems to me, in larger questions of human
security.

00:06:54.800 --> 00:06:59.110
And certainly in going back to Afghanistan
and Pakistan, and when you go to countries

00:06:59.110 --> 00:07:01.879
like that, what is the profound thing that
you see?

00:07:01.879 --> 00:07:07.149
Well, people need a job; they need some connection
to their society; there needs to be some sustainable

00:07:07.149 --> 00:07:08.860
economic development.

00:07:08.860 --> 00:07:14.539
And I think, with Secretary Clinton and President
Obama's help, one of our ideas in that time

00:07:14.539 --> 00:07:19.819
was to focus in on this question of economic
development, sustainable economic development,

00:07:19.819 --> 00:07:24.929
the way the private sector might be involved,
and through that the creation of a New Silk

00:07:24.929 --> 00:07:25.929
Road.

00:07:25.929 --> 00:07:30.159
You're right to say that there was, at that
time, a very interesting change, at least

00:07:30.159 --> 00:07:34.319
from my perspective, in the way that the Chinese
were looking at this question.

00:07:34.319 --> 00:07:39.089
They had made the big investment in the copper
mine, of course, but opened up a conversation

00:07:39.089 --> 00:07:43.169
with us about how to move forward in this
economic area.

00:07:43.169 --> 00:07:46.090
And two things struck me at that time.

00:07:46.090 --> 00:07:51.520
One was we received, one afternoon, my counterpart
from Beijing, the special representative for

00:07:51.520 --> 00:07:56.789
Afghanistan and Pakistan, who was very interested
to find out if we were prepared to cooperate

00:07:56.789 --> 00:07:59.060
on three aid projects in Afghanistan.

00:07:59.060 --> 00:08:04.569
The first time he said that, China was prepared
to do a joint aid project in a third country.

00:08:04.569 --> 00:08:05.960
And we said we were.

00:08:05.960 --> 00:08:12.740
Nurses, training of nurses, training of diplomats,
bringing more agricultural technology to Afghanistan,

00:08:12.740 --> 00:08:15.080
those three projects went ahead.

00:08:15.080 --> 00:08:19.580
We used that, of course, as a basis then to
continue the dialogue and to try to expand

00:08:19.580 --> 00:08:21.959
it in terms of a New Silk Road.

00:08:21.959 --> 00:08:27.770
As you all will recall, Mrs. Clinton was kind
enough to make a speech in Mumbai in 2011

00:08:27.770 --> 00:08:33.950
calling for a New Silk Road to try to connect
Central Asian economies with South Asian economies,

00:08:33.950 --> 00:08:36.530
with Afghanistan and Pakistan in the center.

00:08:36.530 --> 00:08:41.820
And I think that Minister Burki said that
in the previous panel, and so did both other

00:08:41.820 --> 00:08:45.920
panelists, that that would bring, first of
all, some benefit from transit trade but also

00:08:45.920 --> 00:08:50.850
perhaps someday some foreign direct investment
and to integrate a part of the world that's

00:08:50.850 --> 00:08:53.370
very unintegrated economically.

00:08:53.370 --> 00:08:59.420
Charmingly, some weeks after Secretary Clinton
gave this speech, a Chinese representative

00:08:59.420 --> 00:09:04.630
came to see me and said, you know, actually
we like this concept very much but you cannot

00:09:04.630 --> 00:09:06.690
use the term "New Silk Road."

00:09:06.690 --> 00:09:07.690
Really, I said.

00:09:07.690 --> 00:09:08.690
Why might that be?

00:09:08.690 --> 00:09:12.110
Well, because that's our historic view and
vision of the area.

00:09:12.110 --> 00:09:16.200
And I said, actually, I don't mind what we
call it as long as you want to participate

00:09:16.200 --> 00:09:17.200
in this.

00:09:17.200 --> 00:09:18.200
What would you like us to call it?

00:09:18.200 --> 00:09:19.610
He said, how about "historic trade routes"?

00:09:19.610 --> 00:09:24.530
I said, historic trade routes is fine as long
as we're going to do something here together.

00:09:24.530 --> 00:09:30.800
And so this idea of integrating an unintegrated
region, finding ways to produce sustainable

00:09:30.800 --> 00:09:33.640
economic development I think is enormously,
enormously important.

00:09:33.640 --> 00:09:39.190
And we had the good fortune to work with colleagues
in China as they became more interested in

00:09:39.190 --> 00:09:41.490
this, and we encouraged them to do so.

00:09:41.490 --> 00:09:46.360
RUBIN: I just might add that as China got
its own Silk Road Economic Belt, its concern

00:09:46.360 --> 00:09:48.830
about the new-the American New Silk Road decreased,
the terminology.

00:09:48.830 --> 00:09:53.610
GROSSMAN: Well, but as Alyssa and I were talking
about before-and I think, again, Minister

00:09:53.610 --> 00:09:57.930
Burki made this point in the earlier panel-you
know, China, in this case, is thinking long

00:09:57.930 --> 00:09:58.930
term.

00:09:58.930 --> 00:09:59.930
We're thinking short term.

00:09:59.930 --> 00:10:00.930
You know, we had this proposal.

00:10:00.930 --> 00:10:03.020
It was a very good proposal.

00:10:03.020 --> 00:10:06.380
But as those of you who have worked in and
around the U.S. government will know, then

00:10:06.380 --> 00:10:12.860
getting it funded, followed up, part of a
larger integrated whole, you know, is a challenge.

00:10:12.860 --> 00:10:18.230
And so, you know, people, as Alyssa said,
you know, hardly remember this-hardly remember

00:10:18.230 --> 00:10:23.930
this initiative at the time, and I think it
has very much to do with the short-term/long-term

00:10:23.930 --> 00:10:25.780
point that Minister Burki made.

00:10:25.780 --> 00:10:31.940
RUBIN: Evan, I wonder if I could turn to you
to discuss how the United States handles its

00:10:31.940 --> 00:10:41.140
relations with China insofar as they affect
South Asia-India, Pakistan, Afghanistan.

00:10:41.140 --> 00:10:46.800
One thing that the Chinese often say in their-I
suppose it's just-it's four characters or

00:10:46.800 --> 00:10:52.010
something-that we have, you know, common-that
we have competing interests in their front

00:10:52.010 --> 00:10:55.340
yard, common interests in their back yard.

00:10:55.340 --> 00:11:02.730
Yet most of American engagement with China
has been over in the Asia-Pacific region,

00:11:02.730 --> 00:11:06.100
where we have a lot of conflicting interests.

00:11:06.100 --> 00:11:12.060
The growing engagement over Afghanistan, Pakistan,
and South Asia therefore is kind of outside

00:11:12.060 --> 00:11:16.830
the chop lines and bailiwick of the people
who usually deal with China.

00:11:16.830 --> 00:11:20.540
I wonder if you could comment on that and
suggest how the U.S. government might better

00:11:20.540 --> 00:11:25.670
organize itself to deal with China from the
West as well as from the East.

00:11:25.670 --> 00:11:26.840
MEDEIROS: Thank you.

00:11:26.840 --> 00:11:30.000
It's a great question.

00:11:30.000 --> 00:11:37.030
I'd begin by saying that the issues of South
Asia and Afghanistan-Pakistan have sort of

00:11:37.030 --> 00:11:43.590
moved-during my time at the NSC, moved from
issues of tertiary importance to at least

00:11:43.590 --> 00:11:44.872
secondary importance in the U.S.-China relationship.

00:11:44.872 --> 00:11:51.720
You know, during my six years the U.S.-China
relationship-big, complicated relationship-first

00:11:51.720 --> 00:11:57.150
and foremost it's about converging and diverging
U.S. security and economic interests in the

00:11:57.150 --> 00:11:58.150
Asia-Pacific.

00:11:58.150 --> 00:12:03.590
Of course we have big issues that come up-South
China Sea, North Korea, cybersecurity, renminbi-I

00:12:03.590 --> 00:12:09.191
mean big structural issues related to how
international order is going to evolve, but

00:12:09.191 --> 00:12:17.450
obviously, given U.S. deep involvement in
Afghanistan and Pakistan's interests, I think

00:12:17.450 --> 00:12:22.090
gradually over time the Afghanistan issue
became more important.

00:12:22.090 --> 00:12:28.180
And I can remember, when I started at the
NSC as a director, I was regularly calling

00:12:28.180 --> 00:12:29.620
sort of Marc's old office.

00:12:29.620 --> 00:12:33.500
And then when I became senior director, Dan
Feldman and I talked more frequently.

00:12:33.500 --> 00:12:39.530
And basically it was a process of trying to
encourage the Chinese gradually, over time,

00:12:39.530 --> 00:12:41.910
to get more involved in the issue.

00:12:41.910 --> 00:12:46.920
In the first term, under Ambassador Holbrooke,
the Chinese really were not that interested.

00:12:46.920 --> 00:12:52.120
They saw Afghanistan maybe as an economic
opportunity but they had some problems with

00:12:52.120 --> 00:12:57.420
the Aynak copper mine, and as a result minimized
the degree of their economic involvement.

00:12:57.420 --> 00:13:02.490
Diplomatically they just-I think they saw
it as a pit of quicksand and weren't that

00:13:02.490 --> 00:13:04.000
interested in getting involved.

00:13:04.000 --> 00:13:08.070
Gradually, over time, they recognized there
was more of an opportunity there.

00:13:08.070 --> 00:13:14.690
It actually became, by the end of my time,
sort of in the 2014-2015 time period, a bright

00:13:14.690 --> 00:13:20.940
spot in the relationship, an issue that wasn't
related to the Asia-Pacific proper, where

00:13:20.940 --> 00:13:25.530
the Chinese saw themselves as having an opportunity
to work with the United States.

00:13:25.530 --> 00:13:33.110
It ultimately culminated in this new, interesting
quadripartite process that I believe kicked

00:13:33.110 --> 00:13:35.020
off in January of this year.

00:13:35.020 --> 00:13:36.020
Is that right?

00:13:36.020 --> 00:13:37.020
I've sort of lost touch.

00:13:37.020 --> 00:13:38.020
RUBIN: Formally, yes.

00:13:38.020 --> 00:13:39.020
MEDEIROS: Yeah.

00:13:39.020 --> 00:13:40.020
RUBIN: Yeah.

00:13:40.020 --> 00:13:41.790
MEDEIROS: I guess there's a coordination process
and then there's a contact group as well.

00:13:41.790 --> 00:13:48.040
That, to me, is extraordinary, the fact that
it was very difficult to get China, in 2009,

00:13:48.040 --> 00:13:54.530
2010, to even talk about Afghanistan with
the United States in a sustained way to actually

00:13:54.530 --> 00:13:58.340
sponsoring a quadripartite agreement.

00:13:58.340 --> 00:14:03.320
In terms of the organization of U.S. government
policy toward China, I mean-and if there's

00:14:03.320 --> 00:14:07.190
a better way to integrate Afghanistan-the
simple answer is, not really.

00:14:07.190 --> 00:14:15.250
One of the-one of the issues with China is-or
China policy is that it's so diverse.

00:14:15.250 --> 00:14:20.790
It touches so many different aspects of U.S.
policy that there's no ideal way to organize

00:14:20.790 --> 00:14:23.540
China policy to better bring in Afghanistan.

00:14:23.540 --> 00:14:28.779
I mean, really it's about the top leadership
of the U.S.-the president, the secretary of

00:14:28.779 --> 00:14:33.779
state, the national security advisor-telling
their top advisers that Afghanistan is an

00:14:33.779 --> 00:14:39.660
important priority in the U.S.-China relationship
and have that be driven by the NSC.

00:14:39.660 --> 00:14:46.540
I mean, when I was working on China policy,
I mean, it was-I mean, I was constantly pulled

00:14:46.540 --> 00:14:47.900
in multiple different directions.

00:14:47.900 --> 00:14:54.170
In any given day you could be talking about
trade policy, renminbi policy, cybersecurity,

00:14:54.170 --> 00:14:55.390
South China Sea.

00:14:55.390 --> 00:14:59.850
I mean, it's such a big, diversified, complex
relationship.

00:14:59.850 --> 00:15:07.890
There's no ideal way to organize it, but having
sort of streamlined NSC with senior directors

00:15:07.890 --> 00:15:12.270
that are well-coordinated, making sure that
they're regularly talking to their counterparts

00:15:12.270 --> 00:15:16.020
at the State Department I think is as good
a model as there is.

00:15:16.020 --> 00:15:21.240
Trying to create sort of a czar I think just
sort of-adding more layers and more people

00:15:21.240 --> 00:15:23.140
generally is not a good thing.

00:15:23.140 --> 00:15:28.140
Hiring good people, giving them very clear
direction and priorities from the top, I generally

00:15:28.140 --> 00:15:29.990
think works better.

00:15:29.990 --> 00:15:31.190
RUBIN: Thank you.

00:15:31.190 --> 00:15:37.290
Alyssa, in addition to this project you also
direct a project here about U.S.-India relations,

00:15:37.290 --> 00:15:38.589
I believe.

00:15:38.589 --> 00:15:42.720
And one of the points that we heard repeatedly
this morning, which one doesn't hear very

00:15:42.720 --> 00:15:50.529
often but one hears more often now and then,
is about the potential benefits to India-China

00:15:50.529 --> 00:15:56.100
cooperation, economically and even potentially
perhaps on security issues, despite-although

00:15:56.100 --> 00:16:00.890
obviously they have the big issue about the
border and the 1962 war.

00:16:00.890 --> 00:16:07.370
The U.S. has developed a more intensive relationship
with India.

00:16:07.370 --> 00:16:15.460
I wonder if you could comment on how-on how
or if the United States could manage and develop

00:16:15.460 --> 00:16:21.700
these two very important relationships in
a way that might encourage some of the tiny

00:16:21.700 --> 00:16:24.200
shoots of cooperation that we have talked
about.

00:16:24.200 --> 00:16:28.130
AYRES: So I think it's pretty interesting
to take a look at what's been unfolding over

00:16:28.130 --> 00:16:30.140
the course of the last three to four years.

00:16:30.140 --> 00:16:37.320
What you see happening throughout the Asian
region is a deepening involvement of smaller

00:16:37.320 --> 00:16:41.710
regional institutions-you could call them
multilaterals; some people use the word "minilateral"

00:16:41.710 --> 00:16:48.320
to describe them-in addition to the kinds
of initiatives that have been undertaken with

00:16:48.320 --> 00:16:49.670
the Istanbul process.

00:16:49.670 --> 00:16:56.050
In our second panel today you heard about
CICA, the Conference on Interaction and Confidence

00:16:56.050 --> 00:16:57.420
Building in Asia.

00:16:57.420 --> 00:17:00.900
This is an organization that I think most
Americans pay almost no attention to.

00:17:00.900 --> 00:17:06.189
We are formally observers, but this is not
a kind of regional institution that we've

00:17:06.189 --> 00:17:09.220
spent a lot of time thinking about our own
strategy toward.

00:17:09.220 --> 00:17:14.689
Well, this is an organization that spans basically
all of Asia.

00:17:14.689 --> 00:17:17.980
I think the UAE, Turkey are also members.

00:17:17.980 --> 00:17:23.899
China is now emphasizing the role that CICA
can play in providing a kind of regional framework,

00:17:23.899 --> 00:17:26.299
possibly a security organization.

00:17:26.299 --> 00:17:28.069
India is very involved with CICA.

00:17:28.069 --> 00:17:30.240
There are other organizations like this as
well.

00:17:30.240 --> 00:17:34.419
The Indian Ocean Regional Association, the
United States became an observer of that I

00:17:34.419 --> 00:17:37.710
believe in 2012, a very new thing for the
United States.

00:17:37.710 --> 00:17:45.879
That's an institution that covers the countries
that are on the littoral of the Indian Ocean.

00:17:45.879 --> 00:17:50.419
There are organizations like the Shanghai
Cooperation Organization, where you now see

00:17:50.419 --> 00:17:54.760
both India and Pakistan having been accepted
for the membership process.

00:17:54.760 --> 00:17:59.409
They're going through that process now this
year.

00:17:59.409 --> 00:18:04.409
There are things happening with the international
financial institutions.

00:18:04.409 --> 00:18:07.299
We heard talk also about the AIIB.

00:18:07.299 --> 00:18:12.590
India is very involved with the AIIB as the
number-two capital contributor.

00:18:12.590 --> 00:18:17.100
The BRICS Bank, the New Development Bank,
India has the presidency of that as well.

00:18:17.100 --> 00:18:22.950
So you see happening not only a kind of bilateral
economic cooperation between India and China

00:18:22.950 --> 00:18:28.769
but a deepening of the kinds of institutions
in which both countries are now playing a

00:18:28.769 --> 00:18:30.309
stronger role with each other.

00:18:30.309 --> 00:18:34.700
So that kind of cooperation is underway and
I'm not sure that we in the United States

00:18:34.700 --> 00:18:38.220
have paid adequate attention to how that track
is unfolding.

00:18:38.220 --> 00:18:43.179
I mean, through the course of working on this
particular project, one of the conclusions

00:18:43.179 --> 00:18:48.580
my colleagues and I have come to is that the
United States ought to be more involved in

00:18:48.580 --> 00:18:51.119
whatever-we are not observers of the SCO.

00:18:51.119 --> 00:18:52.830
I'm not sure why.

00:18:52.830 --> 00:18:54.580
We could be doing more with CICA.

00:18:54.580 --> 00:18:56.960
We are observers of SAARC.

00:18:56.960 --> 00:19:02.539
Could we be developing a more proactive strategy
to thinking about what our observership could

00:19:02.539 --> 00:19:07.639
help us suggest or propose as part of our
own region-wide strategy in the region?

00:19:07.639 --> 00:19:11.649
So those are some of the things that I think
I've been looking at as ways to help deepen

00:19:11.649 --> 00:19:17.120
our own involvement and ability to be a positive,
proactive player in the region.

00:19:17.120 --> 00:19:23.159
RUBIN: Well, if I can just add a little anecdote
about the U.S. level of U.S. involvement in

00:19:23.159 --> 00:19:29.379
CICA, the only time that I was ever at the
same table with President Putin, President

00:19:29.379 --> 00:19:35.720
Nazarbayev, President Erdogan, and President
Karzai was when I and the deputy consul general

00:19:35.720 --> 00:19:41.110
of the United States in Istanbul were present
as the U.S. observers at a CICA meeting.

00:19:41.110 --> 00:19:46.830
So probably we-possibly we have upped our
game since then.

00:19:46.830 --> 00:19:48.419
I hope so.

00:19:48.419 --> 00:19:49.419
(Laughter.)

00:19:49.419 --> 00:19:56.509
Let's just turn to another subject, counterterrorism,
which is also a subject of discussion with

00:19:56.509 --> 00:19:59.399
all of these three countries.

00:19:59.399 --> 00:20:05.850
I don't want to be diverted into a discussion
of our counterterrorism discussions with Pakistan

00:20:05.850 --> 00:20:10.460
in great detail because that could consume
a great deal of time and emotional energy.

00:20:10.460 --> 00:20:15.539
But, Marc, I wonder if I could start with
you and talk about how the counterterrorism

00:20:15.539 --> 00:20:22.710
concerns shaped and could shape the bilateral
relationship and the relations to regional

00:20:22.710 --> 00:20:23.710
questions.

00:20:23.710 --> 00:20:26.389
And in particular, I'll just note one of the
points that hasn't been mentioned, as far

00:20:26.389 --> 00:20:34.119
as I recall, about China's interest in Afghanistan
and in Pakistan, is their concern about extremist

00:20:34.119 --> 00:20:37.250
separatist organizations in western China,
specifically in Xinjiang.

00:20:37.250 --> 00:20:38.789
GROSSMAN: Yeah, thanks a lot.

00:20:38.789 --> 00:20:40.940
Could you just allow me to just make a comment?

00:20:40.940 --> 00:20:41.940
RUBIN: Absolutely.

00:20:41.940 --> 00:20:43.110
GROSSMAN: I don't want to leave the economic
question on this.

00:20:43.110 --> 00:20:48.570
And I promise I'll come back to the counterterrorism,
but the point, I think, of the panel, as both

00:20:48.570 --> 00:20:52.280
of my colleagues and you've talked about,
Barney, is sort of what could the United States

00:20:52.280 --> 00:20:57.489
do to further the kind of integration that
we heard about really in both panels this

00:20:57.489 --> 00:20:58.489
morning?

00:20:58.489 --> 00:21:03.620
And it seems to me that you could really go
through a very specific list of things.

00:21:03.620 --> 00:21:07.330
I mean, you could talk about, for example,
you know, what's a big challenge in Afghanistan

00:21:07.330 --> 00:21:09.419
in terms of getting more investment there?

00:21:09.419 --> 00:21:10.419
Rule of law, right?

00:21:10.419 --> 00:21:13.789
So rule of law ought to be on somebody's agenda
in Afghanistan.

00:21:13.789 --> 00:21:19.629
Now, if you talk about the huge effort that
Dick Holbrooke and others of you made to get

00:21:19.629 --> 00:21:23.510
the Afghan-Pakistan Transit Trade Agreement
done, right, well, there's something that

00:21:23.510 --> 00:21:28.450
needs to be followed up because, as your reports
have shown, you know, you're not nearly using

00:21:28.450 --> 00:21:31.309
the APTTA as the way that it could be done.

00:21:31.309 --> 00:21:36.269
And there's another place where very specific,
practical efforts might be made.

00:21:36.269 --> 00:21:40.619
You know, I think about trying to get some
proof of concept for some of these areas,

00:21:40.619 --> 00:21:46.620
and I think about the TAPI, program, CASA-1000,
where you could actually get some winds in

00:21:46.620 --> 00:21:49.019
this and people would say, yes, this is going
to work.

00:21:49.019 --> 00:21:53.390
I mean, there was a conversation in the previous
panel about whether this was all too na�ve.

00:21:53.390 --> 00:21:57.019
Well, it might be na�ve but, you know, my
view of all these things is you have to be

00:21:57.019 --> 00:21:58.019
for something.

00:21:58.019 --> 00:22:00.639
You have to get up in the morning and try
to do something.

00:22:00.639 --> 00:22:05.029
And so, you know, here's some proof of concept
that might be-that might be useful Istanbul

00:22:05.029 --> 00:22:06.029
follow up.

00:22:06.029 --> 00:22:11.690
And then I think very much a part of the previous
panel is that this economic integration, call

00:22:11.690 --> 00:22:16.760
it what you will-One Belt, One Road, road
belt, you know, New Silk Road, historic trade

00:22:16.760 --> 00:22:22.489
routes, choose your title-is that there are
some areas where China, India, Pakistan, and

00:22:22.489 --> 00:22:25.630
the United States could all be working on
this together.

00:22:25.630 --> 00:22:30.619
I think you have to take Ambassador Prasad's
point from a previous panel seriously, which

00:22:30.619 --> 00:22:35.789
is to say that we need to make sure that India
doesn't feel that this is a strategic, you

00:22:35.789 --> 00:22:40.460
know, effort to surround them but, you know,
there's a lot of ways that get involved with

00:22:40.460 --> 00:22:41.460
this integration.

00:22:41.460 --> 00:22:46.270
It's connected, it seems to me, Barney, exactly
to the issues of counterterrorism, which is

00:22:46.270 --> 00:22:50.489
that, as the previous panel said, you've got
a whole list of issues-and Evan talked a little

00:22:50.489 --> 00:22:56.239
bit about them-in any day of the week on China,
Pakistan, India, the region, but among the

00:22:56.239 --> 00:23:00.820
things, it seems to me, that the countries
could really cooperate on would be a counterterrorism

00:23:00.820 --> 00:23:01.820
strategy.

00:23:01.820 --> 00:23:02.980
You think about Central Asia.

00:23:02.980 --> 00:23:03.980
What are they worried about?

00:23:03.980 --> 00:23:04.980
They're worried about their borders.

00:23:04.980 --> 00:23:06.889
They're worried about who's moving back and
forth.

00:23:06.889 --> 00:23:10.740
So it's easy to say, sure, we'll have a New
Silk Road, but if you're in Kazakhstan, if

00:23:10.740 --> 00:23:14.309
you're in Tajikistan you're saying, but who's
going across my border?

00:23:14.309 --> 00:23:17.989
And so border issues, counterterrorism issues
are hugely important.

00:23:17.989 --> 00:23:23.549
And the other reason they're important is
although security and terrorism doesn't make

00:23:23.549 --> 00:23:27.830
a hundred percent of decisions for people
who are making investment decisions around

00:23:27.830 --> 00:23:29.860
the world, it does play a part, right?

00:23:29.860 --> 00:23:34.190
It does matter, when people look out when
they're making a presentation to their board

00:23:34.190 --> 00:23:39.119
about making an investment in India or Pakistan
or China or any of the countries in the area,

00:23:39.119 --> 00:23:40.419
is it safe?

00:23:40.419 --> 00:23:45.669
And one of the reasons to get on this counterterrorism
question, it seems to me, is to make it safer

00:23:45.669 --> 00:23:50.980
for more investment, more of this kind of
transit, and go forward from there.

00:23:50.980 --> 00:23:58.110
So it's one of those places, it seems to me,
where you could have intersections among various

00:23:58.110 --> 00:23:59.429
countries, and counterterrorism would be high
on my list.

00:23:59.429 --> 00:24:07.620
RUBIN: Well, I might note, I believe, that
China, India, and Russia have an annual trilateral

00:24:07.620 --> 00:24:11.499
meeting on counterterrorism, which is one
of the indicators that China and India feel

00:24:11.499 --> 00:24:14.129
they have some common interests on that.

00:24:14.129 --> 00:24:18.529
Alyssa, I wonder if you could say-talk about
U.S. involvement in both counterterrorism

00:24:18.529 --> 00:24:25.629
with India but also its role in trying to
promote cooperation on counterterrorism between

00:24:25.629 --> 00:24:31.450
India and Pakistan, and what role China might
play in that since China does believe that

00:24:31.450 --> 00:24:35.440
it has some counterterrorist interests in
common with India, apparently.

00:24:35.440 --> 00:24:40.769
AYRES: Well, I think nobody will be surprised
to hear me say that's a tough one.

00:24:40.769 --> 00:24:45.389
Finding a way to cooperate both with India
and Pakistan on counterterrorism issues I

00:24:45.389 --> 00:24:47.850
think at the same time is a tall order.

00:24:47.850 --> 00:24:50.480
I think that will continue to be a challenge.

00:24:50.480 --> 00:24:54.450
On the other hand, I think that there are
strong bilateral dialogues that exist between

00:24:54.450 --> 00:25:00.090
the United States and India and between the
United States and Pakistan on these questions.

00:25:00.090 --> 00:25:04.230
From my own perspective, I think Pakistan
stands the most to gain from tackling terror

00:25:04.230 --> 00:25:11.260
within its country and terrorism that emanates
from its country in bringing about a safer,

00:25:11.260 --> 00:25:17.320
more secure, more stable economic environment,
which it currently does not have, and it could.

00:25:17.320 --> 00:25:21.580
But that is a decision that Pakistani leaders
need to come to, that they would benefit more

00:25:21.580 --> 00:25:30.129
from having a secure place that would become,
you know, an attractive center for investment,

00:25:30.129 --> 00:25:34.210
strategically located, that would be of more
benefit to them than their current external

00:25:34.210 --> 00:25:35.210
policy.

00:25:35.210 --> 00:25:41.809
I think there's no illusions on the part of
Indian leaders, in conversations with the

00:25:41.809 --> 00:25:47.109
United States, that the United States has
any kind of magical power to be able to change

00:25:47.109 --> 00:25:52.600
this dynamic, but you do see happening in
the United States a kind of changing tenor

00:25:52.600 --> 00:25:57.259
of the conversation right now-what you have
seen unfold, for example, with the question

00:25:57.259 --> 00:25:58.710
of F-16s on Capitol Hill.

00:25:58.710 --> 00:26:06.389
I think our lawmakers are now increasingly
focused on what happens in the U.S.-Pakistan

00:26:06.389 --> 00:26:10.539
relationship and is Pakistan doing everything
that it can to be a good partner?

00:26:10.539 --> 00:26:14.210
And being a good international partner means
tackling terrorism.

00:26:14.210 --> 00:26:19.340
This is not the kind of thing that we want
to make sure that we're supporting.

00:26:19.340 --> 00:26:26.289
So you've seen this F-16s deliberation unfold
and now U.S. lawmakers have said, if you would

00:26:26.289 --> 00:26:28.730
like these, they're not going to come with
a U.S. subsidy.

00:26:28.730 --> 00:26:30.570
RUBIN: Nice.

00:26:30.570 --> 00:26:37.529
Evan, there's also been a U.S.-China dialogue
specifically on counterterrorism that I attended

00:26:37.529 --> 00:26:43.309
once, and I found that every U.S.-China Track
2 meeting related to this subject that I've

00:26:43.309 --> 00:26:48.629
attended, someone on the Chinese side accuses
the United States of having a double standard

00:26:48.629 --> 00:26:50.159
on terrorism.

00:26:50.159 --> 00:26:54.149
And sometimes we have hinted back that maybe
China has a double standard on terrorism,

00:26:54.149 --> 00:26:57.489
considering some of its positions on Pakistan.

00:26:57.489 --> 00:27:03.940
I wonder if you could comment on how things
are going in U.S.-China counterterrorism dialogue

00:27:03.940 --> 00:27:09.390
and how that might be affecting also the work
toward peace in Afghanistan in the QCG.

00:27:09.390 --> 00:27:10.390
MEDEIROS: Thanks.

00:27:10.390 --> 00:27:14.970
That's also a very important question, and
I myself have been accused of having a double

00:27:14.970 --> 00:27:20.030
standard toward China on counterterrorism
so I feel your pain.

00:27:20.030 --> 00:27:25.580
So counterterrorism cooperation between the
United States and China is an area where I

00:27:25.580 --> 00:27:30.940
think the U.S. wish there could be more, but
there are just limits because of differing

00:27:30.940 --> 00:27:33.749
views between the U.S. and China on the nature
of the issue.

00:27:33.749 --> 00:27:39.479
The Chinese want to talk about basically one
thing and one thing only, which is terrorism

00:27:39.479 --> 00:27:48.499
stemming from what they believe is, you know,
violent extremism in Xinjiang.

00:27:48.499 --> 00:27:52.950
And they want to talk about certain terrorist
groups in certain ways, but the Chinese aren't

00:27:52.950 --> 00:27:58.600
really willing to share much information with
the United States or have a more substantial

00:27:58.600 --> 00:28:06.080
dialogue beyond the threat they see coming
from Xinjiang.

00:28:06.080 --> 00:28:11.139
So it's one of these issues where it's-there's
a lot of potential but it's really never been

00:28:11.139 --> 00:28:16.539
realized because of what I see as a very,
very narrow way the Chinese look at the issue.

00:28:16.539 --> 00:28:18.860
And to be honest, it's somewhat distorted
as well.

00:28:18.860 --> 00:28:23.830
I think a lot of the instability-social instability
problems and the violence the Chinese have

00:28:23.830 --> 00:28:33.610
in Xinjiang is not sort of promoted from sort
of Islamic fundamentalism in the Middle East.

00:28:33.610 --> 00:28:40.340
It's due to social problems stemming from
the way the Chinese government handles ethnic

00:28:40.340 --> 00:28:41.850
Uyghurs in Xinjiang.

00:28:41.850 --> 00:28:47.369
And so simply, given differing U.S. and Chinese
views on that issue, it's very difficult.

00:28:47.369 --> 00:28:49.739
That said, I mean, China-there is terrorism
in China.

00:28:49.739 --> 00:28:51.220
There is a real problem.

00:28:51.220 --> 00:28:58.460
I hope that this dialogue can expand but it's
pretty thin and pretty limited.

00:28:58.460 --> 00:29:05.120
That said, I think that the concerns that
China have are an important part of the equation

00:29:05.120 --> 00:29:08.899
for their involvement in Afghanistan, in particular.

00:29:08.899 --> 00:29:16.190
So the way I see it in sort of the Afghanistan-Pakistan
context is it's a necessary but not a sufficient

00:29:16.190 --> 00:29:22.999
motivation to keep the Chinese involved, again
because of the nature of their concerns are

00:29:22.999 --> 00:29:24.080
actually quite narrow.

00:29:24.080 --> 00:29:27.970
But let me make a broader strategic point
that I didn't get a chance to make in my opening

00:29:27.970 --> 00:29:33.869
comments, which is, given the fact that the
Chinese views on Afghanistan have evolved

00:29:33.869 --> 00:29:38.139
especially as the U.S. has withdrawn and the
fact that the Chinese have been willing to

00:29:38.139 --> 00:29:42.389
put more skin in the game, is a net positive
for the U.S.-China relationship.

00:29:42.389 --> 00:29:48.539
Sort of in the grand scheme of the relationship,
it's a relationship in which both sides will

00:29:48.539 --> 00:29:53.820
forever be groping forward for some kind of
new strategic modus vivendi for what drives

00:29:53.820 --> 00:29:59.970
the relationship, because there are both impulses
for competition and impulses for cooperation

00:29:59.970 --> 00:30:04.479
cutting across both security and economic
issues.

00:30:04.479 --> 00:30:08.609
Both sides are trying to figure out what the
other is all about and which aspect-cooperation

00:30:08.609 --> 00:30:10.830
or competition-defines the relationship.

00:30:10.830 --> 00:30:16.929
So the more that there are opportunities,
especially on security and diplomatic issues,

00:30:16.929 --> 00:30:21.330
to highlight where our interests converge
and where we can cooperate, that's generally

00:30:21.330 --> 00:30:22.330
a good thing.

00:30:22.330 --> 00:30:27.539
I mean, when I was at the NSC we were constantly
searching for ways to highlight that we meant

00:30:27.539 --> 00:30:32.549
what we said when we sort of welcomed China's
rise as a responsible power that can play

00:30:32.549 --> 00:30:35.820
an active role in international relations.

00:30:35.820 --> 00:30:41.019
And Afghanistan, though it didn't begin like
that at the beginning of the Obama administration,

00:30:41.019 --> 00:30:46.519
evolved into one where the Chinese were willing
to take, you could even say, more of a leadership

00:30:46.519 --> 00:30:47.519
role.

00:30:47.519 --> 00:30:48.519
And that's a good thing.

00:30:48.519 --> 00:30:49.830
It's something that the U.S. should applaud.

00:30:49.830 --> 00:30:55.419
I will have to say as a footnote, though,
I'm dying at some point in my life to meet

00:30:55.419 --> 00:31:01.080
Afghan diplomats that have been trained by
both the United States and China and to see

00:31:01.080 --> 00:31:04.799
what their worldview looks like, because I
personally can't fathom it.

00:31:04.799 --> 00:31:05.799
(Laughter.)

00:31:05.799 --> 00:31:08.639
RUBIN: Well, there are such people now-
MEDEIROS: That's right, there are.

00:31:08.639 --> 00:31:09.639
Correct.

00:31:09.639 --> 00:31:10.639
RUBIN: Yes, thanks to his efforts.

00:31:10.639 --> 00:31:13.999
GROSSMAN: I've been treated by nurses who
have had a similar-have had a similar background.

00:31:13.999 --> 00:31:14.999
(Laughter.)

00:31:14.999 --> 00:31:18.289
MEDEIROS: So Western medicine and Eastern
medicine simultaneously?

00:31:18.289 --> 00:31:21.190
So an IV while you're chewing on roots or
something?

00:31:21.190 --> 00:31:22.190
(Laughter.)

00:31:22.190 --> 00:31:26.490
RUBIN: Well, it's almost-it's really time
for the questions, but I would be derelict

00:31:26.490 --> 00:31:31.029
if I didn't ask Marc for his views on one
particular thing, which is of course we have

00:31:31.029 --> 00:31:36.609
been talking about the QCG, which is now the
official format for trying to seek a political

00:31:36.609 --> 00:31:44.229
settlement either with the Taliban or with
Pakistan, depending on whom you ask.

00:31:44.229 --> 00:31:50.049
And at the moment, as we all know, it is stalled.

00:31:50.049 --> 00:31:57.960
I wonder if you could-of course it's something
that post-dates your tenure, but a lot of

00:31:57.960 --> 00:32:00.519
the stuff that you did laid the groundwork
for it.

00:32:00.519 --> 00:32:09.539
I wonder if you could comment on how the United
States and China might cooperate more effectively

00:32:09.539 --> 00:32:13.769
to bring Afghanistan and Pakistan together
in that process and also get the Taliban to

00:32:13.769 --> 00:32:14.769
the table.

00:32:14.769 --> 00:32:15.769
(Laughter.)

00:32:15.769 --> 00:32:17.929
GROSSMAN: I see Dan Feldman back there.

00:32:17.929 --> 00:32:19.950
I might leave this to him.

00:32:19.950 --> 00:32:20.950
(Laughter.)

00:32:20.950 --> 00:32:22.381
Well, I had a chance to-
RUBIN: Well, he left office just for that

00:32:22.381 --> 00:32:23.381
reason.

00:32:23.381 --> 00:32:24.381
(Laughter.)

00:32:24.381 --> 00:32:27.059
GROSSMAN: No, I had a chance to listen to
the very first panel and, you know, I think

00:32:27.059 --> 00:32:32.720
that there's a proper amount of skepticism
about what happens in a peace process in Afghanistan

00:32:32.720 --> 00:32:39.220
and Pakistan, but it does seem to me, as Evan
said, that little by little by little this

00:32:39.220 --> 00:32:44.479
recognition that a secure, stable, prosperous
Afghanistan inside of a secure, stable, prosperous

00:32:44.479 --> 00:32:49.759
region is in everybody's interest, and particularly
in the interests of China, and I'd say, to

00:32:49.759 --> 00:32:52.919
Alyssa's point as well, very much in the interests
of Pakistan.

00:32:52.919 --> 00:32:57.440
But I think that's why you see the evolution
of Chinese thinking in this regard.

00:32:57.440 --> 00:33:01.940
And I'm not expert in this but, you know,
as we stepped out from Secretary Clinton's

00:33:01.940 --> 00:33:07.330
speech to the Asia Society in February of
2011 to see whether it might be possible to

00:33:07.330 --> 00:33:12.149
find out if the Taliban were interested in
having some kind of conversation, well, more

00:33:12.149 --> 00:33:14.399
and more interest from the Chinese side.

00:33:14.399 --> 00:33:15.399
Why?

00:33:15.399 --> 00:33:20.869
Because that was a path to getting security
and stability in some kind of capacity in

00:33:20.869 --> 00:33:25.240
Afghanistan, not just for the economic questions
but, as you rightly said, for the strategic

00:33:25.240 --> 00:33:26.679
questions as well.

00:33:26.679 --> 00:33:31.049
And so what came first as curiosity then came
ultimately to interest.

00:33:31.049 --> 00:33:35.519
And I think what you see now is an effort
to try to be part of the process.

00:33:35.519 --> 00:33:42.399
And so sitting from the outside, you know,
to see that China and the United States and

00:33:42.399 --> 00:33:47.409
Pakistan and Afghanistan can sit together
and start to try to have some kind of a construct

00:33:47.409 --> 00:33:51.360
to me is a very important and positive thing.

00:33:51.360 --> 00:34:00.090
As I say, this idea of Afghanistan for everyone
in the region as secure, stable, prosperous

00:34:00.090 --> 00:34:03.979
inside of a secure, stable, prosperous region
is to everyone's benefit.

00:34:03.979 --> 00:34:09.760
And one of the paths to get there is to bring
some kind of political solution to Afghanistan,

00:34:09.760 --> 00:34:13.750
not for us to say what it ought to be, not
for Chinese people to say what it ought to

00:34:13.750 --> 00:34:14.750
be.

00:34:14.750 --> 00:34:17.179
Afghans have to talk to other Afghans about
the future of Afghanistan.

00:34:17.179 --> 00:34:21.150
They'll come to this conclusion, but the more
that we can encourage them to do so, and if

00:34:21.150 --> 00:34:27.540
we're assisted by and joined by countries
like China and India, all to the better.

00:34:27.540 --> 00:34:28.690
RUBIN: OK.

00:34:28.690 --> 00:34:31.400
Look, either of you want to comment on that
further?

00:34:31.400 --> 00:34:32.400
MEDEIROS: I completely agree.

00:34:32.400 --> 00:34:33.400
RUBIN: OK.

00:34:33.400 --> 00:34:34.400
AYRES: Yeah, I agree.

00:34:34.400 --> 00:34:35.400
RUBIN: OK.

00:34:35.400 --> 00:34:38.760
And you mentioned, of course, appropriate
skepticism about peace processes, which I

00:34:38.760 --> 00:34:44.720
also don't object to as long as it is balanced
by appropriate skepticism about war processes.

00:34:44.720 --> 00:34:47.720
Now I will open the floor.

00:34:47.720 --> 00:34:52.600
Please wait for the microphone, give your
name and affiliation, ask a brief question.

00:34:52.600 --> 00:34:53.640
Thank you.

00:34:53.640 --> 00:34:57.940
GROSSMAN: It must be getting to be lunchtime.

00:34:57.940 --> 00:34:58.940
(Laughter.)

00:34:58.940 --> 00:35:00.140
RUBIN: Hmm.

00:35:00.140 --> 00:35:03.800
Well, I could coerce someone.

00:35:03.800 --> 00:35:05.770
AYRES: Use the Socratic method.

00:35:05.770 --> 00:35:06.770
(Laughter.)

00:35:06.770 --> 00:35:07.770
RUBIN: Yes.

00:35:07.770 --> 00:35:10.170
Well, I'm not sure whom to coerce, though.

00:35:10.170 --> 00:35:13.230
Dan, you're sitting in the very, very rear.

00:35:13.230 --> 00:35:17.680
FELDMAN: Yeah, but you've got a full hour
with me coming up.

00:35:17.680 --> 00:35:18.680
(Laughter.)

00:35:18.680 --> 00:35:22.920
RUBIN: You're sitting in the very rear, trying
to avoid being noticed.

00:35:22.920 --> 00:35:27.300
And you have a huge amount of experience on
all of this.

00:35:27.300 --> 00:35:33.650
Would you like-can you think of something
that you'd like further elaborated on?

00:35:33.650 --> 00:35:35.960
(Laughter.)

00:35:35.960 --> 00:35:41.600
FELDMAN: I mean, again, we-I don't want to
steal thunder from the next session, but I

00:35:41.600 --> 00:35:47.520
would certainly concur with much of-virtually
everything I just heard.

00:35:47.520 --> 00:35:54.750
And what we sought to do over the last two
years, three years since Marc was leading

00:35:54.750 --> 00:35:59.920
the SRAP office was very much in alignment
with all of this: the continued quest for

00:35:59.920 --> 00:36:05.190
regional integration, the importance of the
economic piece, and certainly this potential

00:36:05.190 --> 00:36:06.190
role that China has.

00:36:06.190 --> 00:36:12.020
And so I think in terms of potential peace
processes, at the end of the day the very

00:36:12.020 --> 00:36:16.010
open question is what's going to most incentivize
the Taliban?

00:36:16.010 --> 00:36:20.830
And no one around that table can ultimately
do that, but bringing together these parties

00:36:20.830 --> 00:36:27.590
is certainly a net benefit and one that was
very strategic on our part from the very beginning

00:36:27.590 --> 00:36:28.590
of this administration.

00:36:28.590 --> 00:36:32.760
And I was going to note this in our session.

00:36:32.760 --> 00:36:38.980
The very first delegation that I led, at Ambassador
Holbrooke's request, in December of 2009 was

00:36:38.980 --> 00:36:44.752
to China when, at the time-and I think-I can't
remember if you were on that one or a separate

00:36:44.752 --> 00:36:45.752
one-
GROSSMAN: No.

00:36:45.752 --> 00:36:50.260
FELDMAN: -but where they refused to have the
words "Afghanistan" or "Pakistan" in the title

00:36:50.260 --> 00:36:52.320
of the conference or anywhere in any of the
written materials.

00:36:52.320 --> 00:36:56.690
It was just a very general dialogue on South
Asia.

00:36:56.690 --> 00:37:02.750
And so, given what has happened, especially
in the kind of glacial timeframe that China

00:37:02.750 --> 00:37:09.370
usually operates within, the fact that we've
had some of these joint cooperative programs

00:37:09.370 --> 00:37:13.190
and can see the fruits of that, whether in
diplomats or nurses in Afghanistan, the fact

00:37:13.190 --> 00:37:19.160
that they've gone from an observer status
in part of our SRAP group to much more active

00:37:19.160 --> 00:37:23.850
participant, the fact that we've had our first
trilateral between U.S. and China on Afghanistan,

00:37:23.850 --> 00:37:28.460
and now this architecture and framework with
these quadrilateral parties on reconciliation

00:37:28.460 --> 00:37:34.820
is the way that any sort of sustainable resolution
that may occur has to be rooted in.

00:37:34.820 --> 00:37:39.740
When that may occur, what may ultimately incentivize
it, is anyone's guess I think at this point,

00:37:39.740 --> 00:37:44.270
but it is the necessary prerequisite architecture,
I think, for something long term and successful.

00:37:44.270 --> 00:37:49.390
RUBIN: Well, thank you for that comment, which
I forced out of you.

00:37:49.390 --> 00:37:51.510
I think there is-
AYRES: Can I just add to that?

00:37:51.510 --> 00:37:52.510
RUBIN: Yeah, sure.

00:37:52.510 --> 00:37:56.740
AYRES: I do think that one of the issues that
we face as the United States is that many

00:37:56.740 --> 00:38:02.970
in the region feel a sense of American uncertainty
about what our future plans will be for our

00:38:02.970 --> 00:38:05.360
involvement in the region.

00:38:05.360 --> 00:38:10.340
We've seen that unfold with the question about
troop presence in Afghanistan.

00:38:10.340 --> 00:38:16.620
We now have a highly unusual campaign process
going on this year where I think there are

00:38:16.620 --> 00:38:21.280
some other questions that countries are asking
about what U.S. foreign policy will look like.

00:38:21.280 --> 00:38:27.230
None of us can answer that, but I think if
we do focus on creating institutional mechanisms

00:38:27.230 --> 00:38:34.070
for sustained U.S. involvement, whether through
already standing institutions, institutions

00:38:34.070 --> 00:38:38.930
we have helped create and shepherd, like the
Istanbul process, designed specifically to

00:38:38.930 --> 00:38:45.430
provide that mechanism for engagement and
talking about stability, getting those confidence-building

00:38:45.430 --> 00:38:50.900
mechanisms continued, that will at least send
a signal that we are not simply short-term

00:38:50.900 --> 00:38:58.860
thinkers, that we do care about ensuring that
Afghanistan no longer returns to a state of

00:38:58.860 --> 00:39:03.370
insecurity and a problem for the rest of the
region, and that we care a lot about trying

00:39:03.370 --> 00:39:09.490
to shepherd the process of connectivity that
we have said we are trying to shepherd.

00:39:09.490 --> 00:39:13.710
RUBIN: Evan, do you want to add anything?

00:39:13.710 --> 00:39:14.710
OK.

00:39:14.710 --> 00:39:18.960
I've got two over there, first in the back,
and then, Sadanand, you're number two.

00:39:18.960 --> 00:39:19.960
OK.

00:39:19.960 --> 00:39:21.730
Q: Hi.

00:39:21.730 --> 00:39:26.550
Simon Henderson, the Washington Institute
for Near East Policy.

00:39:26.550 --> 00:39:32.600
I live here, I'm an American, but I can't
help noticing that there's been no mention

00:39:32.600 --> 00:39:40.110
of Britain, even in historical terms-(laughter)-nor
in Europe.

00:39:40.110 --> 00:39:44.150
Now, is there no role for Europe?

00:39:44.150 --> 00:39:52.720
I mean, certainly in Pakistan and India it's-but
from my own experience, both countries are

00:39:52.720 --> 00:39:59.850
staffed by people who think in British bureaucratic
terms rather than in American bureaucratic

00:39:59.850 --> 00:40:00.850
terms.

00:40:00.850 --> 00:40:09.990
And tangentially to this, is there any role
for anyone in the Middle East in this?

00:40:09.990 --> 00:40:16.920
And again, historically, I recall that Saudi
Arabia and the Emirates, along with Pakistan,

00:40:16.920 --> 00:40:21.400
were the sole countries which recognized the
earlier Taliban regime.

00:40:21.400 --> 00:40:22.400
RUBIN: Thank you.

00:40:22.400 --> 00:40:23.400
Go ahead.

00:40:23.400 --> 00:40:25.820
GROSSMAN: Well, Simon, first of all, thank
you for your question.

00:40:25.820 --> 00:40:29.890
I don't know if you were here for the previous
panel, but you-Britain came into a fair amount

00:40:29.890 --> 00:40:36.520
of blame from before, so you were-(laughter)-you
were absolutely mentioned in the previous

00:40:36.520 --> 00:40:40.130
panel about what you'd left behind or what
you'd not left behind.

00:40:40.130 --> 00:40:46.040
Let me first of all say that in terms of Europe
and-in terms of Europe, I think there is an

00:40:46.040 --> 00:40:48.970
enormous supportive role that can be played
here.

00:40:48.970 --> 00:40:55.040
I mean, if you consider the effort that was
made in 2011, 2012 to set up Istanbul, Bonn,

00:40:55.040 --> 00:40:58.311
Chicago, and Tokyo, well, what did that involve?

00:40:58.311 --> 00:41:02.780
It involved European countries, both NATO
countries and EU countries, and others as

00:41:02.780 --> 00:41:03.780
well.

00:41:03.780 --> 00:41:10.420
One of, I think, Dick Holbrooke's great accomplishments,
as Dan said, was to put together this international

00:41:10.420 --> 00:41:15.450
consultative group, contact group on Afghanistan,
and European countries played an enormous

00:41:15.450 --> 00:41:16.540
role in that.

00:41:16.540 --> 00:41:20.750
I don't think-and Barney, I hope, would agree
with me-that we would not have been able to

00:41:20.750 --> 00:41:25.910
succeed even a bit in terms of laying some
of the foundations were it not for the close

00:41:25.910 --> 00:41:29.620
relationship between the United States and
Germany on this issue.

00:41:29.620 --> 00:41:34.290
And for me also, I think it's important to
remember here-you know, if you look around

00:41:34.290 --> 00:41:38.870
this room, you get a lot of former government
people-government, government, government-but

00:41:38.870 --> 00:41:44.160
an enormous part of whether this is going
to be successful or not on the economic side

00:41:44.160 --> 00:41:46.450
is whether the private sector is prepared
to get involved.

00:41:46.450 --> 00:41:53.030
And I think European companies in that sense
might be also extremely important.

00:41:53.030 --> 00:42:00.310
The point about the Middle East, also very
much so-a lot of investment from the UAE into

00:42:00.310 --> 00:42:01.310
Pakistan, for example.

00:42:01.310 --> 00:42:06.920
And the UAE in particular were great supporters
of the effort we were making in Afghanistan

00:42:06.920 --> 00:42:09.380
to try to get people some jobs.

00:42:09.380 --> 00:42:10.940
RUBIN: Anything?

00:42:10.940 --> 00:42:11.940
OK.

00:42:11.940 --> 00:42:13.150
AYRES: I agree, yeah.

00:42:13.150 --> 00:42:14.150
RUBIN: One point.

00:42:14.150 --> 00:42:17.080
On the Middle East, you didn't mention Iran.

00:42:17.080 --> 00:42:22.271
We actually need Iran in Afghanistan, and
particularly not so much for the Taliban but

00:42:22.271 --> 00:42:27.260
for keeping the government together, which
is to say for keeping the Bonn agreement in

00:42:27.260 --> 00:42:28.630
effect.

00:42:28.630 --> 00:42:31.370
OK, Sadanand.

00:42:31.370 --> 00:42:33.020
Q: Thank you.

00:42:33.020 --> 00:42:35.990
I'm Sadanand Dhume with the American Enterprise
Institute.

00:42:35.990 --> 00:42:37.630
And my question is for Evan.

00:42:37.630 --> 00:42:43.850
And I want to get back to a comment that Barney
mentioned, the idea of the Chinese double

00:42:43.850 --> 00:42:45.620
standard when it comes to terrorism.

00:42:45.620 --> 00:42:50.470
And this is something that has been very much
in the news, in India particularly, over the

00:42:50.470 --> 00:42:55.340
last month or so because the Chinese have
effectively blocked Indian efforts to put

00:42:55.340 --> 00:42:59.460
Masood Azhar of Jaish-e-Mohammed on a U.N.
list.

00:42:59.460 --> 00:43:07.920
So my question to you is do you see China's
perceptions toward terrorism emanating from

00:43:07.920 --> 00:43:12.380
Pakistan changing thanks to One Belt, One
Road?

00:43:12.380 --> 00:43:17.180
Will it stop turning what Indians perceive
as a blind eye towards the problem?

00:43:17.180 --> 00:43:23.240
And secondly, what role can the U.S. specifically
play, if any, in nudging that process along?

00:43:23.240 --> 00:43:26.270
MEDEIROS: That's a hard question.

00:43:26.270 --> 00:43:34.680
So I think Chinese views, concerns about terrorism
emanating from Pakistan making its way to

00:43:34.680 --> 00:43:42.010
Xinjiang provide sort of an incremental motivation
for investments in One Belt, One Road.

00:43:42.010 --> 00:43:49.280
So I think, generally speaking, that's a good
thing, I mean, in the sense that the Chinese

00:43:49.280 --> 00:43:54.500
feel that they have both a political and a
security interest in paying attention to Pakistan

00:43:54.500 --> 00:43:55.900
and Afghanistan.

00:43:55.900 --> 00:44:03.320
I think that that will hopefully promote smart,
sustainable types of investment using this

00:44:03.320 --> 00:44:07.790
big pot of money that Xi Jinping has identified.

00:44:07.790 --> 00:44:12.450
I don't know whether or not that would shape
their views of terrorism.

00:44:12.450 --> 00:44:20.860
I think, more broadly, what is most likely
to shape Chinese views of terrorism is just

00:44:20.860 --> 00:44:28.620
simply the fact that China, Chinese people,
Chinese companies are all over the world now,

00:44:28.620 --> 00:44:32.540
whether it's the Middle East or Latin America.

00:44:32.540 --> 00:44:38.320
The Chinese people are just simply more vulnerable
to acts of terrorism globally.

00:44:38.320 --> 00:44:45.410
And that has sort of opened up this entire
new space, almost a new discipline, within

00:44:45.410 --> 00:44:51.070
the Chinese foreign policy apparatus focused
on basically sort of citizen services.

00:44:51.070 --> 00:44:57.200
And in fact, the Chinese foreign minister
is much more active and public about defending

00:44:57.200 --> 00:44:59.690
the interests of Chinese citizens abroad.

00:44:59.690 --> 00:45:09.270
So I think that is-the fact that you have
more Chinese citizens active and present internationally,

00:45:09.270 --> 00:45:15.520
the fact that they're vulnerable to all sorts
of terrorism, not just terrorism from extremists

00:45:15.520 --> 00:45:21.010
in Xinjiang, probably over time is making
the Chinese more mindful of that particular

00:45:21.010 --> 00:45:25.780
threat, because the foreign ministry now has
to respond to them to be shown to be responsive

00:45:25.780 --> 00:45:28.630
to the needs of Chinese citizens abroad.

00:45:28.630 --> 00:45:35.690
So I think that the Chinese views maybe will
evolve more due to that.

00:45:35.690 --> 00:45:42.830
Basically they're vulnerable to a diversity
type of terrorist threats, not just one specific

00:45:42.830 --> 00:45:43.830
one.

00:45:43.830 --> 00:45:52.640
But I don't-I can't think of any particular
example of how that new vulnerability or emerging

00:45:52.640 --> 00:46:01.150
vulnerability is changing China's positions
and policies on terrorism per se.

00:46:01.150 --> 00:46:03.790
RUBIN: Thanks.

00:46:03.790 --> 00:46:06.670
GROSSMAN: Frank Wisner back there.

00:46:06.670 --> 00:46:10.270
RUBIN: Oh, Frank, right back there, behind
you.

00:46:10.270 --> 00:46:11.600
Q: Barney, thank you.

00:46:11.600 --> 00:46:15.420
Alyssa, your comments-
RUBIN: Why don't you introduce yourself?

00:46:15.420 --> 00:46:19.960
Q: Frank Wisner, law firm of Squire Patton
Boggs.

00:46:19.960 --> 00:46:27.350
Alyssa, your comments on regional integration
sparked me to try a thought on you and see

00:46:27.350 --> 00:46:28.350
if it makes sense.

00:46:28.350 --> 00:46:36.120
As I listened to you, it strikes me that the
most powerful single integrator on the international

00:46:36.120 --> 00:46:45.610
scene today: the emergence of our mega regional
trade blocks-TTIP for Europe, TPP for Asia.

00:46:45.610 --> 00:46:51.690
And while I recognize we've got a lot of trouble
in convincing the American public to follow

00:46:51.690 --> 00:46:58.260
those through to their logical conclusion,
I'm struck by the fact that South Asia is

00:46:58.260 --> 00:47:05.510
notably absent in the development of these
mega blocks, that both India, Pakistan-but

00:47:05.510 --> 00:47:13.380
Bangladesh and the others-face really severe
problems if the block, particularly TPP, moves

00:47:13.380 --> 00:47:21.780
forward without India's-Pakistan's inclusion:
loss of trade and loss of opportunity.

00:47:21.780 --> 00:47:31.260
But even more important is the loss of the
integrative capacity of TPP, of bringing economies

00:47:31.260 --> 00:47:36.060
and political leaderships together to define
a path forward.

00:47:36.060 --> 00:47:44.920
Now, if that makes sense to you, and it certainly
does to me, then isn't that a policy line

00:47:44.920 --> 00:47:52.090
the United States should be thinking hard
about rather than resisting bringing the Indias

00:47:52.090 --> 00:47:54.210
into TPP?

00:47:54.210 --> 00:48:00.380
Think about what is an evolutionary path forward
that would bring the region more squarely

00:48:00.380 --> 00:48:03.920
into this integrative mechanism.

00:48:03.920 --> 00:48:11.990
There is to be an APEC meeting in Lima in
November in which the question of membership

00:48:11.990 --> 00:48:13.590
will be open.

00:48:13.590 --> 00:48:19.880
None of the South Asians are in the process
of applying that as another step, but also

00:48:19.880 --> 00:48:21.420
an integrative measure.

00:48:21.420 --> 00:48:30.540
Doesn't this represent, as opposed to other
fora, the most powerful single way we could

00:48:30.540 --> 00:48:35.900
help influence the integrative processes in
South Asia and the rest of Asia?

00:48:35.900 --> 00:48:36.900
RUBIN: Thank you.

00:48:36.900 --> 00:48:39.110
Alyssa, would you like to answer that?

00:48:39.110 --> 00:48:41.740
AYRES: I don't think I could have put that
better.

00:48:41.740 --> 00:48:43.940
I agree with you 100 percent.

00:48:43.940 --> 00:48:50.550
I think that we've had some trouble in Washington
seeing how we could use the promise of a larger

00:48:50.550 --> 00:48:58.730
and expanded Asia-wide trade arrangement to
help pull India into thinking about what would

00:48:58.730 --> 00:49:03.050
be in its interest to do to try to join that
grouping.

00:49:03.050 --> 00:49:08.640
We have not worked the way I think we should
be working to try to bring India into APEC,

00:49:08.640 --> 00:49:10.740
which would be a necessary first step.

00:49:10.740 --> 00:49:13.460
All the TPP members are APEC members.

00:49:13.460 --> 00:49:18.050
We haven't begun to even think about this
with respect to smaller economies in South

00:49:18.050 --> 00:49:19.050
Asia.

00:49:19.050 --> 00:49:21.380
You mentioned Bangladesh, which is a big exporter.

00:49:21.380 --> 00:49:27.090
Pakistan could benefit from becoming a more
active economic power and trader.

00:49:27.090 --> 00:49:28.890
So I agree with everything you just said.

00:49:28.890 --> 00:49:33.950
I think we've got some political work to do
in our own capital to think about how these

00:49:33.950 --> 00:49:41.590
could be great strategic tools to help bring
that sense of regional economic connectivity

00:49:41.590 --> 00:49:44.460
and integration to the region, looking more
broadly throughout Asia.

00:49:44.460 --> 00:49:46.380
MEDEIROS: Barney, can I come in on this?

00:49:46.380 --> 00:49:48.140
So I have a question for you, Alyssa.

00:49:48.140 --> 00:49:52.800
I was in the administration when we had this
debate about whether or not to bring India

00:49:52.800 --> 00:49:58.630
in APEC, and one of the arguments that certainly
prevailed at a particular time was you bring

00:49:58.630 --> 00:50:01.680
India in and you drop the standards way down.

00:50:01.680 --> 00:50:10.630
So how do you-how do you address the issue
of, because of India's rather sort of nativist

00:50:10.630 --> 00:50:15.750
tendencies on trade and investment, would
radically change an agreement?

00:50:15.750 --> 00:50:19.750
So I think about when people often ask me
to compare TPP and RCEP.

00:50:19.750 --> 00:50:26.650
The reason why RCEP is going so slow and its
coverage is so limited is because of India.

00:50:26.650 --> 00:50:33.350
So how do you-how do you find the right balance,
because you want a high-quality-I mean, the

00:50:33.350 --> 00:50:39.160
reason why TPP will be so transformational
is not only because there's very high tariff

00:50:39.160 --> 00:50:41.940
coverage-or tariff liberalization coverage.

00:50:41.940 --> 00:50:42.940
That's good.

00:50:42.940 --> 00:50:45.130
But really what's significant is the rulemaking
component.

00:50:45.130 --> 00:50:50.310
The SOE disciplines government procurement
and then of course labor, environmental, et

00:50:50.310 --> 00:50:51.310
cetera.

00:50:51.310 --> 00:50:53.020
So how do you address those issues?

00:50:53.020 --> 00:50:56.470
AYRES: India is not presently ready for TPP
and I am not trying to make that argument.

00:50:56.470 --> 00:50:57.470
MEDEIROS: Right.

00:50:57.470 --> 00:50:58.470
AYRES: So it's a long way.

00:50:58.470 --> 00:50:59.470
MEDEIROS: Completely understand.

00:50:59.470 --> 00:51:02.470
AYRES: And, frankly, if you talk to people
in India, there are a lot of different views

00:51:02.470 --> 00:51:03.740
about trade agreements-
MEDEIROS: Sure.

00:51:03.740 --> 00:51:09.970
AYRES: -but you'll be hard-pressed to find
a strong voice in India to say, we've got

00:51:09.970 --> 00:51:13.610
to radically and quickly continue our reforms
so we can get into TPP.

00:51:13.610 --> 00:51:15.330
I mean, you just don't hear that very often.

00:51:15.330 --> 00:51:17.170
In fact, you hear sort of the opposite.

00:51:17.170 --> 00:51:20.890
You hear people saying things like, the United
States is trying to exclude India-

00:51:20.890 --> 00:51:21.890
MEDEIROS: Right.

00:51:21.890 --> 00:51:22.890
AYRES: -from this great development.

00:51:22.890 --> 00:51:23.890
MEDEIROS: Sure.

00:51:23.890 --> 00:51:27.770
AYRES: But what I would say is that a good
first step would be getting India into APEC.

00:51:27.770 --> 00:51:30.800
That is not a binding trade negotiating forum.

00:51:30.800 --> 00:51:32.650
That is a community of practice.

00:51:32.650 --> 00:51:38.090
That is a forum in which countries get together,
develop action plans for what more they're

00:51:38.090 --> 00:51:42.700
going to do to uphold their commitment, which
they're required to uphold, committing to

00:51:42.700 --> 00:51:45.220
free and open trade.

00:51:45.220 --> 00:51:49.510
And India in APEC-and Indian officials have
said very publicly that they would like to

00:51:49.510 --> 00:51:55.240
be in APEC-they would be required to come
up with plans and show the rest of the APEC

00:51:55.240 --> 00:52:00.340
membership what they would be doing to fulfill
those commitments and keep expanding their

00:52:00.340 --> 00:52:02.670
markets and make it free and open to greater
trade.

00:52:02.670 --> 00:52:03.670
So I'm not saying-
MEDEIROS: So-

00:52:03.670 --> 00:52:09.390
AYRES: So I'm saying that I think we see a
process of, step one, become part of an organization

00:52:09.390 --> 00:52:15.970
that has advanced trade and investment across
Asia, work with India in that context, and

00:52:15.970 --> 00:52:20.460
then look down the line to a roadmap for what
a binding agreement would look like, and it

00:52:20.460 --> 00:52:21.460
has proposed that.

00:52:21.460 --> 00:52:27.260
MEDEIROS: So the argument is that APEC would
socialize India away from its current tendency-relatively

00:52:27.260 --> 00:52:28.520
protectionist tendencies.

00:52:28.520 --> 00:52:29.590
Is that the argument?

00:52:29.590 --> 00:52:31.850
AYRES: That is a way to put that.

00:52:31.850 --> 00:52:32.850
(Laughter.)

00:52:32.850 --> 00:52:33.850
Yes.

00:52:33.850 --> 00:52:34.850
MEDEIROS: How would you put it?

00:52:34.850 --> 00:52:35.850
How would you-no, I'm curious.

00:52:35.850 --> 00:52:36.850
I mean, I'm trying-
AYRES: Yeah.

00:52:36.850 --> 00:52:37.850
MEDEIROS: I'm no India expert.

00:52:37.850 --> 00:52:40.280
RUBIN: Let's let everyone-you think about
it and you can come back later with that.

00:52:40.280 --> 00:52:42.470
In fact, it may be in her article.

00:52:42.470 --> 00:52:43.470
(Laughter.)

00:52:43.470 --> 00:52:44.940
Do you want to add to that?

00:52:44.940 --> 00:52:45.940
GROSSMAN: No.

00:52:45.940 --> 00:52:46.940
RUBIN: OK.

00:52:46.940 --> 00:52:47.980
Jonah, in the back there.

00:52:47.980 --> 00:52:49.800
Q: Thanks, Barney.

00:52:49.800 --> 00:52:56.800
I'd like to get back to the point that Evan
raised about President Xi's pledge of $46

00:52:56.800 --> 00:53:03.070
billion for Pakistan infrastructure, and whether
this really could be transformational for

00:53:03.070 --> 00:53:05.220
regional stability.

00:53:05.220 --> 00:53:11.850
As you all know, we in the U.S. had a big,
bold infrastructure plan to-Kerry-Lugar-Berman.

00:53:11.850 --> 00:53:18.150
We tripled our economic aid and many of us
thought and hoped that this would be truly

00:53:18.150 --> 00:53:19.370
transformational.

00:53:19.370 --> 00:53:24.550
We had real difficulty spending that money
in a way that was transformational.

00:53:24.550 --> 00:53:29.180
And I'm sure some people would go even further
than that-than that critique.

00:53:29.180 --> 00:53:34.820
What's the view of the panel first on whether
President Xi is actually going to deliver

00:53:34.820 --> 00:53:42.680
$46 billion, and second, if he does, whether
that actually will have a transformational

00:53:42.680 --> 00:53:43.680
impact?

00:53:43.680 --> 00:53:47.520
RUBIN: All right, well, you directed it initially
to Evan but it's clearly for everyone.

00:53:47.520 --> 00:53:51.210
But do you want to start by talking about
whether they actually will pay it?

00:53:51.210 --> 00:53:58.540
MEDEIROS: Chinese pledges of big assistance
are pretty common these days.

00:53:58.540 --> 00:54:02.870
My recommendation would be, read the fine
print.

00:54:02.870 --> 00:54:08.160
I think a lot of it will depend on Pakistan's
absorptive capacity.

00:54:08.160 --> 00:54:12.640
And while, Jonah, I don't know the specifics
of that particular pledge, I would imagine

00:54:12.640 --> 00:54:20.130
a large portion of it is financing from China's
own development banks, Ex-Im Bank, CDB, et

00:54:20.130 --> 00:54:25.740
cetera, Silk Road Fund, related to-that have
to be spent in employing Chinese state-owned

00:54:25.740 --> 00:54:29.200
enterprises that are flush with overcapacity.

00:54:29.200 --> 00:54:33.860
That would be my guess but that's preliminary
because, again, I haven't read the details

00:54:33.860 --> 00:54:34.860
of this.

00:54:34.860 --> 00:54:40.410
But the structures of these packages are important,
so that's why I recommend we all read the

00:54:40.410 --> 00:54:41.410
fine print.

00:54:41.410 --> 00:54:45.860
RUBIN: Either of you want to comment on the
challenges of disbursing that kind of money

00:54:45.860 --> 00:54:46.860
in Pakistan?

00:54:46.860 --> 00:54:47.860
AYRES: I'll pass.

00:54:47.860 --> 00:54:48.860
(Laughter.)

00:54:48.860 --> 00:54:49.860
RUBIN: OK.

00:54:49.860 --> 00:54:50.860
GROSSMAN: I will.

00:54:50.860 --> 00:54:51.860
RUBIN: OK, please do.

00:54:51.860 --> 00:54:57.900
GROSSMAN: First of all, let me say that $46
billion-it strikes me that even if it turns

00:54:57.900 --> 00:55:01.150
out to be half that amount of money, it's
still a lot of money.

00:55:01.150 --> 00:55:06.020
And so I think there is this possibility of
doing something transformative, which is why,

00:55:06.020 --> 00:55:10.190
as Ms. Su said earlier, in the panel, I hope
that's someplace actually that Chinese and

00:55:10.190 --> 00:55:15.370
the United States could work together, and
as Ambassador Prasad said, with India as well.

00:55:15.370 --> 00:55:20.840
Second, I think the question on Kerry-Lugar-Berman,
of which, you know, I was a huge supporter-and

00:55:20.840 --> 00:55:25.840
Dan Feldman is the person who kept it going
for many, many years, at least on the administration

00:55:25.840 --> 00:55:28.950
side-you have sort of a philosophical question,
Jonah, here.

00:55:28.950 --> 00:55:34.420
I mean, I think that there's aid and there's
aid.

00:55:34.420 --> 00:55:38.250
And one of the things that the United States
tried to do through Kerry-Lugar-Berman-very

00:55:38.250 --> 00:55:44.540
properly, it seems to me-was do all kinds
of projects-infrastructure projects, health

00:55:44.540 --> 00:55:47.130
projects, education projects-and that's all
good.

00:55:47.130 --> 00:55:50.530
And Robin Raphel worked on this very hard
as well.

00:55:50.530 --> 00:55:52.332
The Chinese, though, what are they doing?

00:55:52.332 --> 00:55:53.730
They're focused on questions of infrastructure.

00:55:53.730 --> 00:55:56.520
And I think that makes a big difference.

00:55:56.520 --> 00:56:01.100
So my first post in the foreign service was
Pakistan, it turns out, in 1977 and '79.

00:56:01.100 --> 00:56:04.090
What do most Pakistanis remember about U.S.
aid at that time?

00:56:04.090 --> 00:56:05.370
The Tarbela Dam, right?

00:56:05.370 --> 00:56:11.520
That's what they remember, a huge infrastructure
project that changed a lot of agriculture

00:56:11.520 --> 00:56:12.770
and life in Pakistan.

00:56:12.770 --> 00:56:17.980
And so I think the issue here is if the Chinese,
in their One Belt, One Road, are focused on

00:56:17.980 --> 00:56:21.470
infrastructure, they'll find a way to spend
it.

00:56:21.470 --> 00:56:26.450
The absorptive capacity will be there because
people need this infrastructure, and therefore

00:56:26.450 --> 00:56:30.020
has a chance of being transformative.

00:56:30.020 --> 00:56:35.140
And that's just a philosophical difference,
I would bet, between what they intend to do

00:56:35.140 --> 00:56:37.880
and what we tried to do, and try to do, through
Kerry-Lugar-Berman.

00:56:37.880 --> 00:56:39.060
RUBIN: Well, thanks.

00:56:39.060 --> 00:56:44.730
Well, we're just about near the end, so let
me-let me just ask the panelists if you have

00:56:44.730 --> 00:56:49.370
any final words before we go on to the next
one?

00:56:49.370 --> 00:56:50.370
Evan?

00:56:50.370 --> 00:56:51.370
MEDEIROS: No.

00:56:51.370 --> 00:56:56.670
AYRES: My primary concern when we think about
U.S. engagement and policy toward this region

00:56:56.670 --> 00:57:01.250
is that we continue to keep the South Asia
part of Asia high on our priority, high on

00:57:01.250 --> 00:57:06.280
our agenda, so in the sense of when we think
of Asia, this is a part of Asia.

00:57:06.280 --> 00:57:12.080
Let's kind of rebalance our rebalance and
ensure that our involvement-we have a commitment

00:57:12.080 --> 00:57:18.060
to staying the course, that the region understands
that we have that commitment, and that we'll

00:57:18.060 --> 00:57:24.520
continue to place a high priority on making
sure that we're active and involved and engaged

00:57:24.520 --> 00:57:25.520
in our diplomacy.

00:57:25.520 --> 00:57:26.520
RUBIN: Thank you.

00:57:26.520 --> 00:57:30.390
GROSSMAN: I think it's great that these last
two panels have focused on the economic side

00:57:30.390 --> 00:57:34.130
of this because, as you said previously, it
sometimes gets lost.

00:57:34.130 --> 00:57:38.940
And I think that thinking long term and thinking
simultaneously and trying to actually have

00:57:38.940 --> 00:57:44.890
five or six or eight or 10 practical things
that can be done to promote the Silk Road,

00:57:44.890 --> 00:57:46.670
or whatever it's called, is hugely, hugely
important.

00:57:46.670 --> 00:57:52.990
You know, I go back to-(coughs)-excuse me-a
quotation from, you know, Robert Kaplan's

00:57:52.990 --> 00:57:57.480
book "Monsoon," you know, where it says getting
this Afghanistan economic piece right is the

00:57:57.480 --> 00:58:01.990
key then to getting the piece right in all
of South Asia and therefore of Asia.

00:58:01.990 --> 00:58:05.550
So it's enormously important and I'm glad
we were able to spend some time on it this

00:58:05.550 --> 00:58:06.550
morning.

00:58:06.550 --> 00:58:10.480
RUBIN: Well, let me thank all the panelists
and the participants for the questions.

00:58:10.480 --> 00:58:16.690
We're now going to adjourn for lunch, which
I presume is out there, and return here to

00:58:16.690 --> 00:58:19.570
start promptly on the next session at 1:00
p.m.

00:58:19.570 --> 00:58:20.570
Thank you.

00:58:20.570 --> 00:58:22.050
GROSSMAN: Thank you.

00:58:22.050 --> 00:58:23.540
AYRES: Thank you.

00:58:23.540 --> 00:58:24.030
(END)

